1
00:00:00,841 --> 00:00:08,690
Welcome to Morocco's regions, part 1 of 1. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:19,232
We work through 12 of the Morocco regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:19,901 --> 00:00:25,102
Let's bring Tangier-Tetouan-Al Hoceima into view.

4
00:00:27,455 --> 00:00:45,383
Tangier-Tetouan-Al Hoceima is one of the twelve regions Morocco divided itself
into in 2015, and it sits in the north, reaching from Tangier on the Strait of
Gibraltar along the Mediterranean coast to the Algerian border, with the Rif
mountains inland.

5
00:00:46,037 --> 00:00:59,772
The strait is only about 14 kilometres wide here, so Spain can be seen across
the water on a clear day, and inland the Rif mountains hold Chefchaouen, whose
houses are washed in blue.

6
00:01:00,427 --> 00:01:08,276
Remember the narrow gap of water with Spain just across it, and the blue town
waiting in the hills behind the strait.

7
00:01:08,955 --> 00:01:12,624
Let's bring Rabat-Salé-Kénitra into view.

8
00:01:16,122 --> 00:01:32,106
Rabat-Salé-Kénitra is the north-western region on the Atlantic coast, where
the Bou Regreg river meets the sea below Rabat, with Tangier-Tetouan-Al
Hoceima to the north and Casablanca-Settat to the south.

9
00:01:32,763 --> 00:01:44,837
Rabat takes its name from the Arabic ribat, a fortified monastery or camp, and
the Hassan Tower above the river is the unfinished minaret of a mosque begun
in 1195.

10
00:01:45,496 --> 00:01:52,021
Remember the tower that was never finished, rising over the river mouth with
the Atlantic just below it.

11
00:01:52,701 --> 00:01:55,336
Let's bring Fès-Meknès into view.

12
00:01:59,813 --> 00:02:12,433
Fès-Meknès is the interior region of the north, between the Rif mountains and
the Middle Atlas, touching five other regions, with the walled cities of Fez
and Meknes at its heart.

13
00:02:13,086 --> 00:02:25,323
Fez holds the University of al-Qarawiyyin, founded in 859 and claimed to be
the oldest still working in the world, and Roman Volubilis with its mosaics
lies to the west of the city.

14
00:02:25,978 --> 00:02:32,967
Remember the oldest university in the world, in the walled city that sits
between the Rif and the Middle Atlas.

15
00:02:33,653 --> 00:02:35,615
Let's bring Oriental into view.

16
00:02:41,121 --> 00:02:49,260
Oriental is the north-eastern region, sitting along the Algerian border with
the Moulouya river running north through it and out to the Mediterranean.

17
00:02:49,923 --> 00:02:59,257
Its name simply means the eastern region, and the land border with Algeria has
been closed to crossings since 1994.

18
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:06,398
Remember the eastern region with a sealed border on one side and a river
running out to the Mediterranean on the other.

19
00:03:07,174 --> 00:03:10,843
Let's bring Casablanca-Settat into view.

20
00:03:18,302 --> 00:03:30,795
Casablanca-Settat is the north-western region on the Atlantic coast, with
Casablanca on the shore and Settat inland to the south, between
Rabat-Salé-Kénitra and Marrakech-Safi.

21
00:03:31,458 --> 00:03:44,682
Casablanca means “white house”, from the Portuguese Casa Branca, and the
Hassan II Mosque stands partly out over the sea with a minaret of about 210
metres.

22
00:03:45,332 --> 00:03:52,066
Remember the white house on the Atlantic and the mosque built out over the
water with a very tall tower.

23
00:03:52,746 --> 00:03:55,637
Let's bring Béni Mellal-Khénifra into view.

24
00:03:59,913 --> 00:04:12,916
Béni Mellal-Khénifra is an inland region of the north-central country, lying
between Casablanca-Settat, Fès-Meknès and Marrakech-Safi across the Middle
Atlas.

25
00:04:13,569 --> 00:04:21,580
Khouribga here is the centre of Morocco’s phosphate mining, and the country
holds most of the world’s known phosphate reserves.

26
00:04:22,241 --> 00:04:29,149
Remember the sons of Mellal and the waterfall of Ouzoud dropping into a Middle
Atlas gorge.

27
00:04:29,829 --> 00:04:33,022
Let's bring Marrakech-Safi into view.

28
00:04:37,017 --> 00:04:47,083
Marrakech-Safi is the western region, running from the Atlantic coast at Safi
up to Marrakech at the foot of the High Atlas, with Drâa-Tafilalet to the
east.

29
00:04:47,748 --> 00:05:00,542
Marrakech probably gave the country its name, from a Berber phrase usually
explained as “land of God”, and its Jemaa el-Fna square of storytellers and
musicians is on the UNESCO list.

30
00:05:01,198 --> 00:05:07,885
Remember the square that fills with storytellers and musicians at dusk, in the
city that gave Morocco its name.

31
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:11,195
Let's bring Drâa-Tafilalet into view.

32
00:05:15,758 --> 00:05:27,356
Drâa-Tafilalet is the south-eastern interior region, running down from the
Atlas into the Sahara, with Oriental to the north and Souss-Massa to the
south-west.

33
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:40,814
The Aït Benhaddou village of red clay stands here, a World Heritage Site that
has doubled for many films, and the Alaouite dynasty comes from the Tafilalet
oasis.

34
00:05:41,464 --> 00:05:49,347
Remember the red clay fortress village that fills film sets, in the valley and
oasis that give the region its name.

35
00:05:50,028 --> 00:05:53,093
Let's bring Souss-Massa into view.

36
00:05:57,492 --> 00:06:11,273
Souss-Massa is the south-western region facing the Atlantic, with Agadir on
the shore and the Souss and Massa rivers running down to it, next to
Guelmim-Oued Noun in the south.

37
00:06:11,934 --> 00:06:22,685
Argan trees grow almost nowhere else on earth and their oil is the region’s
speciality, while Agadir was rebuilt after an earthquake flattened it in 1960.

38
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:30,933
Remember the argan trees that grow nowhere else, and the coast town that was
rebuilt after the 1960 earthquake.

39
00:06:31,606 --> 00:06:34,752
Let's bring Guelmim-Oued Noun into view.

40
00:06:38,707 --> 00:06:49,411
Guelmim-Oued Noun is the southern Atlantic region below Souss-Massa, a strip
of coast and desert between the mountains and the Sahara, with Sidi Ifni on
the shore.

41
00:06:50,067 --> 00:07:01,027
Guelmim is known as the gateway to the desert, and the Tan-Tan Moussem, a
gathering of nomadic peoples, is on the UNESCO list of intangible heritage.

42
00:07:01,681 --> 00:07:08,798
Remember the gateway to the desert, and the fair at Tan-Tan where nomadic
peoples gather.

43
00:07:09,471 --> 00:07:14,800
Let's bring Laâyoune-Sakia El Hamra into view.

44
00:07:17,076 --> 00:07:28,930
Laâyoune-Sakia El Hamra is the northern part of Western Sahara, the Atlantic
territory south of Morocco that the map draws as part of the country, reaching
inland from the coast to the Algerian border.

45
00:07:29,579 --> 00:07:40,492
Tarfaya in this region is where the pilot and writer Antoine de Saint-Exupéry
ran a mail airfield in 1927 and 1928, on the desert shore.

46
00:07:41,145 --> 00:07:49,841
Remember the springs that give Laâyoune its name and the red channel of a dry
river valley, with the mail airfield on the coast.

47
00:07:50,524 --> 00:07:55,423
Let's bring Dakhla-Oued Ed-Dahab into view.

48
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:12,912
Dakhla-Oued Ed-Dahab is the southern part of Western Sahara, the Atlantic
territory south of Morocco, a long strip down the coast below Laâyoune-Sakia
El Hamra reaching the Mauritanian border.

49
00:08:13,568 --> 00:08:26,188
Its name means “river of gold” in Arabic, the Spanish called the area Rio de
Oro, and Dakhla on its long peninsula was called Villa Cisneros under Spain.

50
00:08:26,844 --> 00:08:33,926
Remember the river of gold that the Spanish called Rio de Oro, with the
kite-surfing peninsula of Dakhla at the tip.

51
00:08:40,012 --> 00:09:06,364
12 regions of Morocco, one for each target of the map: Tangier-Tetouan-Al
Hoceima, Rabat-Salé-Kénitra, Fès-Meknès, Oriental, Casablanca-Settat, Béni
Mellal-Khénifra, Marrakech-Safi, Drâa-Tafilalet, Souss-Massa, Guelmim-Oued
Noun, Laâyoune-Sakia El Hamra and Dakhla-Oued Ed-Dahab.

52
00:09:07,020 --> 00:09:17,086
Remember the shape of Morocco from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
